Technical Report: Mode 5 IFF / ADS-B Link Debugging
Hardware: Pluto SDR (TX) ↔ SMA cable ↔ ZedBoard FMCOMMS3 AD9361 (RX)
Software: MATLAB Communications Toolbox SDR, demo_comm.m → test_adsb_link.m

Executive Summary
demo_comm.m failed due to five independent bugs that compounded each other. None was hardware-related — the SMA cable and both radios functioned correctly throughout. The problems were entirely in the signal generation, filtering, and decoding software. test_adsb_link.m was created to isolate and fix each bug on the simpler ADS-B foundation before layering Mode 5 crypto back on top.

Bug 1 — PlutoSDR Rejected Real-Valued Waveform
Symptom:

Root Cause:
The TX waveform was built as:

MATLAB's isreal() returns true for both of these when the imaginary part is identically zero — it optimises the storage back to a real array internally. The Pluto driver checks isreal() and throws before transmitting.

Fix:

A 1e-12 imaginary component is 100 dB below the signal and completely inaudible to the DAC, but it prevents MATLAB from collapsing the type.

Bug 2 — Sample Rate Too Low for ADS-B Pulse Timing (Critical)
Symptom: After fixing the complex issue, the demodulator produced random bits (BER ~50%) regardless of signal strength. The calibration probe showed max=0.003 (noise floor) at 4 MSPS despite identical gain settings that produced max=0.31 at 12 MSPS.

Root Cause:ADS-B uses PPM (Pulse Position Modulation) with a fundamental pulse width of 0.5 µs. At 4 MSPS:

Parameter	4 MSPS	12 MSPS
Samples per µs	4	12
Samples per 0.5 µs pulse	2	6
Samples per bit period (1 µs)	4	12
Preamble kernel length	32 samples	96 samples
With only 2 samples per pulse:

The preamble correlation kernel had only 4 active samples out of 32 total → extremely poor SNR in the correlator output
Any filter (highpass, moving average) operates over window lengths that span multiple pulses at once, destroying the pulse structure
The transmitRepeat buffer at 4 MSPS repeated the packet so fast that the RX frame would capture many overlapping repetitions with random phase alignment
Fix: Changed fs = 4e6 → fs = 12e6 (the ITU standard for ADS-B receivers). This gives 6 samples per half-pulse, making the waveform recoverable by correlation.

Bug 3 — Wrong DC Removal Destroyed PPM Bit Energy (Critical)
Symptom: With gains correct and 12 MSPS, BER was still ~39-47%. RECEIVED was always 0000...0000 — every 1 bit decoded as 0. Exactly 44 errors = exactly the number of 1 bits in the test message.

Root Cause:
The decoder pipeline was:

highpass() applied to the magnitude envelope acts as an edge detector (it outputs the derivative of the envelope). For a PPM 1 bit (pulse in the first half = [HIGH HIGH LOW LOW]), the edge detector produces:

Now both halves have equal spike energy → tie → decisions become noise-driven → systematic 50% BER. For the specific test message 8D4840D6... with 44 ones, all 44 ones decoded as zeros → exactly what was observed.

The fix required filtering the complex IQ signal before envelope detection, which removes the AD9361's DC ramp at the source without touching pulse shape:

Option B (moving average) was chosen because highpass() still introduces transient ringing on the 6-sample-wide pulses at 12 MSPS. The 20 µs moving window is 240 samples — wide enough to track the slow AD9361 startup ramp but narrow enough to leave individual 6-sample pulses untouched.

Bug 4 — Gain Range Was Wrong on Both Ends
Symptom — Too Low: max=0.003, BER=50%. The correlator found noise spikes instead of the preamble, producing random data_start values.

Symptom — Too High: max=1.4142 = √2. This is the exact ADC saturation value for a normalized complex signal (|I + jQ|_max = √(1² + 1²) = √2). The signal was clipped to a square wave, destroying all modulation.

Root Cause: SMA cable has ~0 dB path loss. The initial gains (-50 dB TX, 0 dB RX) came from antenna-link assumptions — an antenna link has 60–80+ dB of propagation loss, so those settings were effectively applying -50 dB to a signal that needed +0 dB attenuation. The +20 dB overcorrection (to TX -10 dB, RX +30 dB) pushed 40 dB too far in the other direction, saturating the AD9361 front-end.

Fix: Auto-calibration sweep that measures max(abs(rx)) directly and picks the gain combo that lands the signal in the linear range (0.05–1.0), settling on TX=-20 dB, RX=+10 dB giving max=0.31.

Target amplitude guideline for SMA cable:

max(abs(rx))	Diagnosis
< 0.01	Signal absent / gain far too low
0.01–0.05	Below noise floor — increase RX gain
0.05–0.80	Good linear range
0.80–1.00	Marginal — reduce TX or RX
≥ 1.00	ADC clipping — reduce immediately
= 1.414	Full saturation — both I and Q rails clipped
Bug 5 — Missing cal_data = zrx() Assignment (Typo)
Symptom:

Root Cause: During a refactor of the calibration loop, the line cal_data = zrx(); was deleted while editing the for f = 1:3, zrx(); end flush block above it. The radios were connecting, flushing, and releasing, but the actual calibration capture was never saved. best_max_amp stayed at 0 for all gain settings, triggering the No signal detected error before the test even started.

Fix: Restore the single missing line between the flush loop and release().

Why demo_comm.m Mode 5 DSSS Further Complicated Things
On top of the five bugs above, demo_comm.m added a DSSS (Direct Sequence Spread Spectrum) layer with a length-31 Gold code. At 4 MSPS this meant:

Each chip was round(4e6/1e6) = 4 samples wide — marginally workable
But the DSSS preamble was 2 × 31 × 4 = 248 samples long
The frame size length(tx_interrog) * 3 was only ~3× the waveform, so when data_start was computed incorrectly (due to the same off-by-one and DC bugs), the payload extraction window fell outside the buffer
The crypto validation (sync + CRC-16 + MAC) then rejected the garbled bits — correctly — but the error message "Interrogation FAILED validation" gave no indication that the actual problem was 6 layers deeper in the signal chain.

What test_adsb_link.m Does Differently
Aspect	demo_comm.m	test_adsb_link.m
Modulation	BPSK + DSSS (Gold-31)	Plain PPM (ADS-B standard)
Sample rate	4 MSPS	12 MSPS
DC removal	highpass(abs(IQ))	movmean on envelope
Gains	Fixed (wrong for cable)	Auto-calibration sweep
Validation	Crypto MAC + CRC	Direct bit comparison
Debug output	Pass/fail only	Amplitude stats, SNR, BER, bit-level diff
Complex TX	complex(x, zeros) (failed)	x + 1i*1e-12 (works)
Path Forward to Mode 5
Once test_adsb_link.m consistently shows BER = 0%, the correct sequence to re-introduce Mode 5 is:

Keep 12 MSPS and the movmean decoder — carry these directly into demo_comm.m
Replace PPM with BPSK — simpler to decode at wider bandwidth, better noise immunity
Remove DSSS initially — add it back only after plain BPSK works cleanly
Add CRC-16 alone first — verify framing before adding the MAC layer
Add MAC last — once CRC passes consistently, the crypto will be the only remaining variable

